In article <3291807F.7B99@nt.com>, Graham Weeks <Graham_Weeks@nt.com> writes:
:In the configuration there is a DECNet host which is 
:connected to two lan segments and is acting as a DECNet 
:router. The two interfaces on this host have the same DECNet 
:MAC address - this I believe is probably legal in the DECnet 
:world.

  DECnet Phase IV can be connected to multiple LAN segments if
  the segments are non-bridged or connected via routers, and if
  the system has a routing license.

  DECnet-Plus (DECnet OSI) does not have this LAN connection
  limitation -- unlike Phase IV, the station PROM addresses
  are used as LAN addresses.

