In article <848083183.18771@dejanews.com>, wallis@cs.twsu.edu writes:

:	I have a VAXstation 3100 with a burnt out VR262 monochrome monitor.
:Is there any way to either use a VGA or SVGA monitor (with an adaptor cable) 
:or use a VT100 as an alternate console for this machine?

  The VAXstation 3100 can redirect the OPA0: console to the serial line,
  and there's a switch (labeled S3 -- on _very_ old VAXstation 3100 series
  boxes, it's covered over and you have to take the top off to get at it)
  on the back of the VAXstation 3100 series, and this can be used to toggle
  the processing of the HALT.  Connect the VTxxx terminal to the serial
  line, tap return and/or <BREAK> a few times on the terminal (making
  sure the console speed is set up correctly), BOOT, and off you go...

  The VAXstation 3100 series video designs predate most (all?) VGA and
  SVGA standards, and -- with the synch-on-green RGB interface -- most
  recent VGA and SVGA monitors tend not to work with this synch-on-green
  cabling scheme.

  There are a multitude of old monitors around for these boxes -- the VR160,
  VR260, VR262, VR290, VR299 and a few other video monitors should work with
  the GPX controller (1024x864x60Hz), and the VR319 and VRT19 should work if
  this box has the SPX controller (1280x1024x66Hz or 1024x864x60Hz).

  Be aware that you will want to leave the video controller installed and
  the workstation keyboard connected, even if you connect the `console' to
  the serial line terminal.

  -------------------------- pure personal opinion ---------------------------
