> Hello,
> 
> A quick, simple question from an OSF newbie:  I've noticed that on
> an Alpha running OSF that the space available for non-root users
> on a filesystem is actually about 90% of the real disk space available.
> This is normally fine for most filesystems, but I have a 10GB news
> partition (an advfs filesystem), and it's reserving about 1.5 GB for
> root usage- so, according to df, when I have 8.5 GB of data on this
> disk, the filesystem is "full", and my news server shuts down, etc.
> 
> How can I increase the space available for use- I'd like to reserve
> the 'safety' space to just a couple hundred MB...
> 
> Thanks in advance for any answers, advice, hints, etc.
> 
> tom
> 
> tfischer@rain.fr

Dear Tom,

	you can use 'tunefs -m <minfree>' to change the percentage of
space held back from normal users of a UFS volume, where <minfree>
is defaulted to 10%.

Yours, Hans.




