Subject: Alpha 21164 questions
Date: 1996/11/26
Message-ID: <57fhna$9kr@hisatsinom.cs.arizona.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 200902855
organization: U of Arizona, CS Dept, Tucson
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.arch


Hi,

   I'm doing a report on the 21164 and have not been able
to find the answer to some of the questions in the online
literature or the alpha architecture manual.  Here are
some questions I've assembled:

- two bit history is kept with instructions in the icache
  for branch prediction.  How are these bits used?

- what happens on a branch mispredict?  When is the
  mispredict noticed and what happens to the subsequent
  instructions that have been issued?  What is the
  mispredict penalty?  It looks like the branch penalty
  for correct prediction is zero (no stalls), is this true?

- It looks like it takes two cycles to read D-cache, is this
  true?

- It looks like it takes 4 additional cycles to get a result
  from S-cache if the D-cache misses,  is this correct?
  Is the miss penalty for the I-cache also 4 additional cycles?

- What happens in pipeline stage three for floating point
  operations?  Why are integer register arguments fetched
  in stage three but floating point register not fetched
  until stage four?

- The pipeline diagram in the 21164/233 data sheet (Figure 1, 
  page 4 of my preliminary copy from the web) shows a 
  "Load Data" path into the floating point register file
  but no such line into the integer register file.  The
  arrows out of the Data Cache are also labelled "To
  Floating-Point Unit".  Is this a typo?  Is there
  a similar input into the integer register file?  If not
  why?  and how do integer loads work?

- What is the penalty for ITB and DTB misses?  Are TB
  entries filled by hardware or palcode?

- The instruction pipeline diagram says that conditional
  move or branch operations can be issued the same cycle
  as a compare?  How is this done?

A few questions on the 21264 information I've seen posted:

- What is the global pattern detecting predictor?
  What are the Next line and Set predictors?

- I've read that it is 6-way issue and can retire
  8 instructions.  What is the significance of allowing
  8 instructions to retire in an instruction?
  
- What is the expected perfomance benefit of the
  additional byte and word load/store operations? 

I would also like to know if there are any performance
evaluations available.  In particular I would like to
see a break down of stalls by type (ie. branch mispredict,
cache misses, etc..) for different programs (ideally
the set of Spec95 programs) and an analysis of the CPI. 
I'm also interested in any analysis of the miss address file
and the write buffer.
If anyone has pointers to data like this I would appreciate it.

I'm sure there are many questions I'm forgetting to ask.

Please CC me any news replies as I dont frequent this newsgroup.
Thanks in advance for any replies.  

                                  Tim N.





