From: sharpe@ns.aus.com (Richard Sharpe)
Subject: A neat trick with LSM/Veritas
Date: 1996/11/25
Message-ID: <329a1435.591801@news.adelaide.on.net>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 200702741
content-type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
organization: NS Computer Software and Services Pty Ltd
mime-version: 1.0
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Here is what I think is a neat trick with LSM/Veritas for Digital UNIX
and probably other Unixen.

A customer has a database system that they are finding difficulty in
taking down to do backups, so they started doing on-line backups.

However, the on-line backups were locking users out for too long
during the day, so they took 4 RZ29s and concatenated them into one
16+GB volume using LSM. This is all very easy, and the sort of thing
you are allowed to do without any extra licences with Digital UNIX.

Disks are cheap!

Now, they do their on-line backups to disk, which is fast, then they
copy to tape at their leasure.

Sure, the reliability of the 16+GB volume set is lower than than of
one disk drive (by about a factor of 4), but, by the same token, we
are only using the large volume set for a small portion of the day, so
we should have reduced our risk there.

In addition, if the volume set gives us trouble, we can get a new
drive from Digital under the maintenance agreement and go back to
using tape for that day, or defer backups for a few hours.
Regards
