  Once upon a time, Windows NT did run on the DEC 5000.  It was early in the
  development of Windows NT (I think I was running it before the kickoff
  developers seminar in San Fransisco circa 1992 I think), and the folks on
  the West Coast who did the work and actually demo'ed it were all dispersed
  to the wind when their A/D group was disbanded.

  I had a DEC 5000 running WNT for a while, but eventually bagged it and sent
  the machine to the dumpster when it became clear that Microsoft and DEC were
  not going to productize it... and once I got the AlphaPC I was waiting for.

  It's really not brain surgery.  You just need a system with an ARC console
  (the DEC 5000 had one), someone who knows how to spin a HAL, and someone to
  write all the device drivers for the Turbochannel devices.  It's just a lot
  of work.

  But hey, isn't NT on MIPS dead?  So isn't the question moot?  And please,
  don't ask me for the old code for the DEC 5000.  I no longer have it.




