Martin Douglas <dafis@ppp.test.jccbi.gov> wrote:
>In article <578kqt$b94@camel2.mindspring.com>, <skg1@mindspring.com> writes:
>> Greetings!
>> 
>> We have an Alpha 8420 multi-processor server with 60 gig of disk, 2
>> gig of memory, and 2 TZ877 tape drives. Our DBM is Ingres which allows
>> online data backups, and which is currently configured to use tar to
>> do them. 

Doesn't this cause some application impact though, as the database
either has to lock things or do before images of each page users
modify while backups are in progres. Perhaps it has to do both.
 
>> We dump approximately 16 gig of data several times daily, and are
>> spending 3 hours each time using tar and the TZ877s. My question to
>> you: does a faster method exist for doing a disk-to-tape backup on the
>> Alpha box? Digital says no, that the best you can get is using their
>> TZ887 drives ($12,000 a pop). 

Well, two tapes may do the job, but you could also consider dumping to
disk to allow the users back in quickly and then dump to tape at your
leisure.
 
>> I don't mind 3rd party if it's reliable and fast. I wish HP offered
>> Omniback for a Digital box, but as far as I know they don't. 
>> 
>> If you've heard of anything, please respond to this newsgroup. I
>> monitor it every day. Thanks for your time. 

>>                     skg1@mindspring.com (Steve Grantham)                    
>> 
>I Just taked to a Dec Technical Rep, because we have the same problem.  I do 
>not know what a tz887 tape drive is.  We use 4mm tapes.  Dec has 
>tape/cartridge tape drives that can hold 35 gig or more of data.  Ask about a 
>DLT type backup drive.

Ummm, the TZ887 is DLT.

I currently have a customer that dumps to disk using on-line backups
and then backs up to tape. They find that the impact on users is thus
minimized and the database can stay up longer. The cost of disks is
dropping all the time. You do need LSM of course, but you have
licences for everything you need with DU without the extra LSM licence
unless you want to also mirror the backup volume for extra protection.

What we have done is to create a 16+GB volume called backup that is
mounted as /backup (/dev/vol/rootdg/backup on /backup) which the
online backups then dump to. You could mirror that areas as well to
bring the reliability back up.

>Martin Douglas

