C Robinson <cr@lava.net> writes:

> id Softwares 3D guru Carmack recently announced a Alpha NT version of
> Quake and OpenGL porting (for 3DFX)

Interesting, in particular, since to my knowledge, Quake won't run on
Intel NT.

> Does this mean that a high clock speed Alpha system will be the
> dedicated Quakers platform of choice?

Possibly, if the OpenGL stuff can make use of hardware 3D acceleration,
and more importantly, do all these nasty byte-operations.  The alpha
processor doesn't have them, you see, and this makes the 8-bit frame
buffer of Quake rather suboptimal.  Perhaps the port you mention uses
more colors?  I think that would make sense.

On Linux, you have two versions of Quake, one for DGA, and one for
regular X.  DGA (Direct Graphics Access, I think, lets the processor at
the frame buffer directly.  Or something) actually appears to *slow
down* Quake on the alpha.

> The reason I ask is I am unhappy with my present map start, status bar
> only, 320x200, timerefresh value of 32.5 on my P166. Call me
> insane. ;)

Uh...well, you'll probably end up spending quite a bit of money to top
that, but hey, it's your money, and you'll probably end up with a decent
workstation in the process.

Anyway, if the Linux benchmarks are worth anything to you, I think a
