Michele Michelotto (michelot@lhctb00.cern.ch) wrote:
: Colin Dooley wrote:
: > 
: > Silicon graphics can sell you a machine with an 85Gb/s (with 'G')
: > sustained disk throughput. Don't know what they use it for, I mean
: > this machine can eat a terabyte every 12 seconds... :-S

: 85Gb/s or 85 GB/s ?
: Could you give me more info on this machine?
--
The interconnect inside an O2K is good for ~600MB/sec per I/O enclosure
(yeah, it's rated at 800MB/sec peak or some such marketing number, but
I'm not willing to promise you more than 600.  Remember, I'm the lmbench
guy, the numbers I quote are numbers that you can take to the bank.
I never quote theoretical peak, I only quote stuff that I've done myself
or seen done.  It's frequently possible to do better than what I say
can be done, but it is usually non-trivial to do so.).

You can get an I/O card cage (6 cards) with every two nodes (so one I/O
per 4 processors).  You can put FC cards in that are good for 72MB/sec
per cable; you get two cables per card, and up to 6 cards, so you can get
864MB/sec per I/O enclosure.  That's more than the 600MB/sec I quoted,
so let's leave it at 600.

Oh, yeah, all the links internally are full duplex, so I'm sure that
marketing would quote 1.6GB/sec peak bandwidths.  I personally think
that quoting peak is misleading, but understand the need for that sort
of thing.  As long as everyone else quotes peak, we have to too or
we look bad.  It's like quoting 40MB/sec for ultra SCSI.  People then
stupidly think their disk is going to delvier 40MB/sec.  Yeah, right.

We've announced 128 processor systems, so that is up to 32 I/O card
cages or 19.2GB/sec of possible I/O.  I have no idea how anyone came
up with either 85Gbit or 85Gbyte/sec.  Nor have we ever demonstrated 
a file system running at either speed.  We have demonstrated 700MB/sec
(I haven't seen this, but I believe it, read on) and I'm toying with 
the idea of demoing a 2GByte/sec file system (Sun claimed a 1GB/sec
one using 8 processors and 36 max strat raids).  I'm pretty sure we 
could do 2GB/sec with 25 raids and 1 processor and have enough idle
cycles to run an X demo at the same time.
