One of my frustrations while working for Digital as a sales support
person configuring AlphaServers was the limited number of PCI slots in
all but the 8x00 series.

While the 2100 and 1000 arguably took the right aproach with 7 EISA
slots and 3 PCI (or something like that) because it wasn't clear that
PCI was going to be the success it is today, the 2100A, 1000A and even
the 4100 leave a lot to be desired.

While on paper the 2100A and 1000A both have 7 PCI slots, the fact is
that the last 4 are behind the PCI to PCI bridge: you would not put
anything faster than a 10Mb ethernet controller on them. So you are
limited to 3 PCI slots.

Even the 4100 with two 64-bit PCI busses and 8 total slots only has 5
left after the mandatory SCSI, graphics and Ethernet are configured. 

This does not leave a lot of slots for SCSI controllers on either the
1000A, 2100A or the 4100 and I like to spread my IO around. I have a
customer who has 3 KZPAAs in his 2100 and can only put disks on
existing chains, one of which has 7 devices on it.

When are Digital going to support third party SCSI adapters more, some
of which have dual-channel controllers, eg Adaptec and BusLogic? This
would make for much more slot-efficient configurations and would
relieve Digital from the need to develop its own controllers. In my
view Digital's R&D dollars are better spent developing drivers for
third-boards rather than developing the boards and drivers. 
Multi-channel 100BTx controllers would be valuable as well.

Of course, it may be that while I was at Digital, I missed something. 





