A pre-beta of 64 bit NT in the first half of 1997?  Having seen how large
the mods to VMS were to get 64 bit stuff working, and that on a platform
that is 64 bit only in hardware, I'd be somewhat surprised to see it
so relatively soon. NT was designed with 32 bits in mind, and there is
a heck of a lot of complexity in moving to 64. Also this does not in general
provide a way to get much speed change; I'd expect that NT speed would be
somewhat hurt at first by the need to handle either width and by the
extra book-keeping and memory access needed for 64 bits; its calling
sequences look like they got somewhat bent out of shape for Intel
80x86 compatibility from the bits I've seen. Remember, 64 bit NT is not
being written at DEC (where the VMS experience might conceivably be
available as a consulting thing) but at Microsoft, where many of their
folks are still using 16 bit bits of code (the core of W95, for example,
being old crufty MSDOS). They, like lots of folks, were mightily surprised
when 64-bitness turned out to be able to give, not a few percent better
database performance, but hundreds of times better. And it takes a while
to get everything in synch. VMS 7.0 had the essentials of 64 bit support.
7.1 has more still. More yet may follow. Meanwhile, pre-announcing 64 bitness
is a way to try and get people not to buy what has 64 bitness now. Also
some other vendors announce 64 bit hardware, but their OSs still have 
32 bit addressing, and processors may only be able to get at 32 bits
worth of address space at a time. (Consider Sun...). Maybe each processor
can get at different (or mostly different) chunks of memory, but that
is not what I consider a 64 bit machine or OS. (By the same token, a
pdp11 is a 16 bit machine, not a 22 bit one, even though it could address
22 bit memory addresses.



