Bill Broadley <bill@math.ucdavis.edu> responded to what
>sharpe@ns.aus.com (Richard Sharpe) wrote:
>
>>One of my frustrations while working for Digital as a sales support
>>person configuring AlphaServers was the limited number of PCI slots in
>>all but the 8x00 series.
>>
>>While the 2100 and 1000 arguably took the right aproach with 7 EISA
>>slots and 3 PCI (or something like that) because it wasn't clear that
>>PCI was going to be the success it is today, the 2100A, 1000A and even
>>the 4100 leave a lot to be desired.
>>
>>While on paper the 2100A and 1000A both have 7 PCI slots, the fact is
>>that the last 4 are behind the PCI to PCI bridge: you would not put
>>anything faster than a 10Mb ethernet controller on them. So you are
>>limited to 3 PCI slots.
>>
>>Even the 4100 with two 64-bit PCI busses and 8 total slots only has 5
>>left after the mandatory SCSI, graphics and Ethernet are configured. 
>>
>>This does not leave a lot of slots for SCSI controllers on either the
>>1000A, 2100A or the 4100 and I like to spread my IO around. I have a
>>customer who has 3 KZPAAs in his 2100 and can only put disks on
>>existing chains, one of which has 7 devices on it.
>>
>>When are Digital going to support third party SCSI adapters more, some
>>of which have dual-channel controllers, eg Adaptec and BusLogic? This
>>would make for much more slot-efficient configurations and would
>>relieve Digital from the need to develop its own controllers. In my
>>view Digital's R&D dollars are better spent developing drivers for
>>third-boards rather than developing the boards and drivers. 
>>Multi-channel 100BTx controllers would be valuable as well.
>>
>>Of course, it may be that while I was at Digital, I missed something. 

>Ummm why not just buy the digital 3 fast/wide/diff channels on a single
>pci card?

Well, there are many things you cannot do with the KZESC, the KZPSC
and the newer backplane raid controllers (which are all Mylex boards).
I keep thinking that the KZPSD is the KZPSA replacement, not the KZPSC
replacement. 

Under Digital UNIX, you cannot boot from a JBOD device (re) on one of
the boards, and you don't get much use out of them in ASE or Cluster
situations, as you cannot fail over the RAID sets, and they take up
precious slots. Similarly, you cannot use them to with the HSZ40 to
fail an HSZ40 between SCSI channels.

When you are building a cluster system, with redundent Ethernet, SCSI,
and Memory Channel, you run out of slots even with a 4100. OK, you can
use the first ethernet in the system as one of the redundent ethernet
controllers. Certainly on the 2100A and 1000A (which might not be
supported at this stage for Clusters) you cannot even think of fully
redundent paths between systems.

So, they are good in single machine situations. But then again, in
heavy database situations, where you like to give lots of spindles to
the database, you might not want an extra processor between you and
the database. However, YMMV.

>Do you have numbers to backup your assertion that anything
>behind the bridge is really slow?

Well, no, I don't. However, while I was at Digital, based on feedback
from engineering, and by looking at the SOC for which controllers are
supported after the PCI bridge, I was telling customers not to put
anything faster than 10Mb ethernet after the bridge. I have also seen
customer situations where moving a KZPSC from after the bridge to
before the bridge made it work.

