kilgallen@eisner.decus.org (Larry Kilgallen) wrote:
>In article <E1957w.49A@world.std.com>, "Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>> Ron Natalie <ron@sensor.com> wrote:
>
>>>My guess is that "alpha" is so heavily overused that it's impossible
>>>to get trademark registration on (hence the meaningless AXP).  
>> 
>> Very likely true. Alpha started out as the code name for the 
>> Son-of-PRISM/Son-of-EVAX processor architecture. Hence some wags claim 
>> AXP stands for Almost eXactly Prism.

>Perhaps successor-to-Prism, but I do not believe there is a design
>connection.
>
My esteemed colleague Mr. Kilgallen speaks the truth. The PRISM 
architecture was scalar/vector, and was slated to be available in 32-bit 
(MicroPRISM) and 64-bit (PRISM) versions under the JEWEL architecture 
program.

A decision was made to eschew vector processing and stress superscalar 
processing instead. Note that vectors were all the rage in the mid-80's 
(Cray, Convex, the VAX Vector Architecture, etc) but soon fell out of 
favor. I don't profess to know why, maybe something to do with IBM's 
decision to implement RIOS as a superscalar design.

