In article <bazzoli_tl-1811962224130001@38.11.111.8>, bazzoli_tl@mindspring.com writes:

>I got a VAX 4000-400 in a recent auction lot.  It is a very clean machine
>with 3 FH Hard Drives.  I haven't tried taking it apart to learn more. 
>What kind of machine is this?

It is a VAX architecture system.  It is one three models in the
class using the same packaging.  It is the slowest of the three
models running ~16 times faster than a VAX 11/780 (*).  The starting
configurations of each model supported different amounts of memory
by default, but all support up to 512 MB.


>Server?

Or time sharing system.  It may depend on the license.

>Still supportable item?

The only operating system support you're going to get is OpenVMS,
but it is still a supported system.  11/750s are still supported
systems...

>Are the drives SCSI or a DEC proprietary type?

It appears that the system only supports DSSI drives, which are
DEC proprietary that look a lot like SCSI, but aren't.  Full height
drives will make them one of the RF7x models.  With a terminal
connected to the console port, you should be able get the system
up to a ">>>" console prompt.  From there, I suspect a "show dssi"
command will show the device on the busses and the models.  The
members of the RF7x family are:

RF71 - ~390 MB.
RF72 - 1 GB.
RF73 - 2 GB.

If the drives are 3.5" form factor devices, they are probably RF35s
at 852 MB.

>Any information or pointers to info sources would be appreciated.

For a mid-sized VAX it looks like a pretty good system.  There
may be more information on our FTP server.  Try looking on
ftp://www.digital.com/pub/DEC.  There might be old Systems
and Options catalogs or marketing summaries.  I'm looking at
a printed copy of the November 1992 SOC.

Other odds and ends of interest... The system includes two
DSSI adapters supporting a maximum of 14 disks (7 each) or
other DSSI hosts in place of disks.  It also has a built-in
Ethernet with thick and thinwire connections.  Many systems
included a KZQSA Q-bus SCSI adapter for connecting tapes or
a CDROM.  This adapter never supported disks under OpenVMS.
You probably won't like the results if you try to use disks
on it.

