> It was my understanding that Intel at one time and possibly to this
  Do you mean Microsoft?       ^^^^^
> date still uses a "p-code" interpreter. Many parts of applications
> were interpreted in part to reduce the memory footprint. There was
> allegedly something about the pcode format that made it easier to
> decode on a x86 box.

I recently posted someone else's claims about the role of the p-code.
We need to find out where the allegations in "allegedly" originate.

If the "easier" came from the easy byte addressing or string
instructions in the x86 architechture, would that be a bias--
or would it merely point out that RISC chips were not designed
with highest performance in word processing as the ultimate goal?

It wasn't clear if you were talking about Word of Excel.
Wouldn't one expect most spreadsheets to use a pcode
interpreter?
 
> I noticed a dramatic difference in the performance of Word 6 on a PPC
> Mac and a Windows box. My 80 Mhz PPC 601 was out-paced by a 75 Mhz
> '486. Clearly the hardware was "faster", particularly when running
> apps written by someone else (e.g., Adobe).

Yes, there may be strong biases there, but the MacOS could
also be a limit in the way it allocates memory.  A more telling
example would be how fast Word runs under NT on a PPC machine;
I saw one review on the web that said Word was quite snappy
on a Moto Powerstack (100 MHz 604, I think) under NT.  I think that
suggests there were other aspects of the MacOS port that did
not go well, apart from the RISC processor.

And as we have discussed, there may be vast differences
between the way bit-mapped graphics operations use memory,
vs. the way a word processor uses memory.  Consider a
weighted 5*5 smooth, say with 7 unique multipliers;
on a system with 32 FP registers, all the multipliers,
and 20 of the values needed for the next point, can be held
in the registers; for the remaining 5 items fetched from
memory for each pixel, there is extreme locality of reference.
Compare that with a word search through the snaky path
of a document with headers, footers, figure captions, tables
and cross-references.

The "Adobe" vs. "Microsoft" may be a comparison of two
very different application types, rather than a comparison
of two software companies.



