There was a rumour at one stage that Word and Excel consisted of large
amounts of x86 assembler which were ported in a rush (i.e. in a
non-optimal manner) to Alpha, and that many of the data structures were
byte sized and/or unaligned, making access on a RISC architecture (and
Alpha in particular) considerably slower than it should be. No matter
how good the compiler, bad programming wins every time, and this kind of
programming would be definitely biased towards the x86 platform.
