In article <01bbd735$44417680$ab8d93cf@400139425worldnet.att.net> "A K" <akuebler@worldnet.att.net> writes:
>Does anyone know how fast and how well a DEC Multia with 166MHz 64bit RISC
>Alpha CPU and 24MB RAM runs 95 programs when using FX!32?

It will run Win32 programs functionally correct. You will
not be pleased with the performance of the translation step itself if
you only have 24 Mbyte and you will be not very pleased with the resulting
speed (and no amount of RAM will fix that on the Multia). The Multia
is based on the 21066 Alpha CPU which is the slowest
of all Alpha ever done. It is not competitive even with Pentium CPUs,
It can make a very nice lowcost box under Linux or NT and is attractive
because you can get them very cheap (500-800 $).

>Digital says that with FX!32, a 150MHz Alpha processor will run 95 programs
>at about the speed of a 60Mhz Pentium.  Is this really true?  

I think that there are several statements out, but this particular one
is new to me. Just quoting  Mhz for an Alpha doesn't help, because there
are at least three implementations (soon 5) of Alpha out there which
have different performance characteristics even at the same clock
rate:

	21066	(166-233)		old,slow cheap
	21064	(133-300)		old,medium speed
	21164	(233-500)		current,medium to high speed

The only ones that are competitive to Pentium Pros under FX!32 are
the 21164 ones.

>The 95 programs Digital is talking about mostly extremely popular programs like
>MS Office, other Microsoft products, Lotus, etc.  Or does this also to
>apply games like Quake, Descent, Warcraft, Command and Conquer,
>Mechwarrior, Wing Commander III, Sim City 2000, etc.?   Can this computer
>using FX!32 even run games like this?  What about DOS games?

Windows NT 4.0 with FX!32 will run all Win32 applications through FX!32
(or native) with good performance, Win16 applications and certain
protected mode 32 bit and  DOS applications under emulation (slow performance)
and  will not run most of the games you cited because they won't adhere
to Win32 conventions.

	Sorry,

