Peter Mayne wrote:

> There was a rumour at one stage that Word and Excel consisted of large
> amounts of x86 assembler which were ported in a rush (i.e. in a
> non-optimal manner) to Alpha, and that many of the data structures were
> byte sized and/or unaligned, making access on a RISC architecture (and
> Alpha in particular) considerably slower than it should be. No matter
> how good the compiler, bad programming wins every time, and this kind of
> programming would be definitely biased towards the x86 platform.

We hear this a lot, and I don't see an easy way to determine if
the rumor was more than a rumor.  I found an old message on my
mail heap about this issue, and I'll attach it at the end; 
regrettably, it doesn't offer concrete evidence either way.

As for misalignment:  Wouldn't that hurt the PPro or Pentium as
well as the alpha?  I've certainly found a severe hit for
misaligned doubles on the p5 and p6 class machines.  And can't
one just use the /align or -align keyword in compilation?  I've
done the former with VMS on an alpha, and it seemed to work
just fine.  If a CPU has inherited byte-level instructions from
a CISC ancestry, should that be a fault -- especially when it is
so convenient for word processing?

now that old message:
