ask this question have either decided to go with new terminal servers (the
"cost is no object" bunch) or give up on the idea (the "budgets are frozen"
gang).

  Given that the box's architecture is very well documented, the availability
of good source-code TCP/IP implementations, and good 68K development tools
(gcc) it's not an impossible task. However, every time I've been approached
about it, I've found the customer to be in one of the two groups above and it
never went anywhere.

  That's not to say it won't ever happen - I remember a few years ago DEC con-
tracted with me to do DS200 load/dump code for RSTS/E so they could sell their
customer some new network hardware, even though it would have been cheaper to
just swap the customer's DS200's for something that booted locally, like the
DS700+flash or any of the 3rd-party servers. [Note: this code (in BP2) can be
downloaded from ftp.spc.edu (in the mopsrv directory, as I recall)].

