jsquires@hns.com (Jeff Squires) writes:

> I have the opportunity to purchase a new 21164 for $2,199.  It comes
> with:
> 
> 	233 MHz processor

As somebody pointed out, this could be the 21064 which is quite a bit
slower, or it could even mean the 21066, which is *way* slower.  (Is it
a Multia/UDB?  Those can be had for less than $800 nowadays -- even the
233MHz model.)

> 	3-D video card

The built in TGA, I wonder?  The Digital-provided X server is decent on
it, but it isn't really all that cool for 3D.

> 	WindowsNT
> 	Digital UNIX
> 	FX 32 binary translator (to run x86 32 bit apps)

If you're going to run Linux, you won't need those, and can probably
save a bundle.  In particular, Digital Unix comes with a rather pricey
licence, I think.

> 1. If I am mostly running ms windows (95,NT) apps, how slow is the
> conversion from alpha-->x86 machine code?  My 233 MHz will act like a
> ___________ MHz pentium.

Depends on the type of software you want to run.  A decent Pentium in
most cases, I'd guess.

> 2. Can I install Linux (what's the pain in the neck factor for doing
> this on a DEC vs. an Intel machine?

If the DEC has a PCI bus, it should be a breeze.  Get RedHat 4.0, at
least 3.0.3 was as easy or easier than most other OSes.  If you're
familiar with Unix already, you should have no problems getting the
system up.

> 3. Am I better off with a Pentium 166 for $1,800?

Depends on what you want to do.  If you want a system optimised for
stability, Linux on a Pentium is probably better.  If you want to run
Windows apps, I think I'd rank the OSes as  1) Win95, 2) WinNT, 3)
AlphaNT w/ FX!32 4) P5 Linux w/ Dosemu or WINE.

If you want hack value (but still a very fast and useful system) get the
Alpha with Linux.

> 4. Will the power of the alpha ensure that I won't be replacing it as
> quickly as I'd have to with a pentium 166?

Depends.  The more cutting the edge, the more often it needs
sharpening.  How and when you replace it is your choice -- I still run a
486 at home, and a HP715 at work, and I'm fairly happy.

> 5. Any major sacrifices that I would have to make by not buying a
> Pentium?  (remember, this will be for personal use only)

None, now that you can run Quake on alpha/linux. :-)

Booting into Win95 occasionally, to run those apps or games that won't
work under NT?

~kzm




