I did attend that session, and two things came to mind: Reconfigure time
and Low End system.

Reconfigure time: how long will it take to reconfigure the components of
the Galaxy?  After much uniformed thought, I would say some integral, and
meanigful, multipule of Quantum: say one Quantum for the Conection Manager
to remove the first node from one of the culsters, one Quantum to let the
Lock Manager do just that, one Quantum for the Connection Manager to add
this node to another cluster, one Quantum to schedual a thread.  So four
Quantums. So, OK, uniformed, but then Quntum has some meaning.

Low End system: Will the Galaxy "biased" OpenVMS handle a singe CPU
system, like a desktop workstation?  This was answered to some extent at
the OpenVMS Eng. Q&A session: OpenVMS engineers use OpenVMS workstations
for development because they are the most cost affective machine to do
development on.  Nobody gets thier own TurboLaser :-(   Hopefully the
degenerate case of a single CPU system will not be forgoten, since most
companies and products start out as small: nobody start out as big as,
say, Oracle.


Random thought.
To extend the Galaxy concept the other direction, when a 64-issue Alpha is
relesed, will the scheduler be able to run more than one thread
concurently on the same CPU? i.e. 4 threads with 16 execution units each? 

Well... 
how would the compiler allocate registers?





