A friend and myself tried the pentium overdrive chip upgrade on our DECpc XL 466d2 systems.
Here are the results of this adventure:

1st we updated the NVRAM bios to the current version (V1.23a, from the DEC PC www page).
After removing the 486 CPU chip, we inserted the new pentium overdrive chip, and powered the
system up. It booted, getting past the SCSI disk detection, and hung. We were a little PO'ed
at this point, because DEC and Intel advertize that this system was tested for overdrive 
compatability. We play arround with bios settings, and discover that if we disable ALL CPU
cache, the overdrive chip works, but its SOooooooo slow.

At this point, we give up and call DEC. We sent one of the 2 systems in to service under it 3 year
waranty, DEC keeps the machine for 3 weeks, and sends it back working. They said that there
was an early version of the motherboard (not the daughter card), that did not work well with the
overdrive chip. This is what that did.

	1) Replaced the motherboard (not the daughter CPU card).
	2) Removed the CPU and CPU cache from the daughter CPU card.
	3) Installed my pentium 83mhz overdrive CPU.
	4) Disabled the CPU cache settings in the NVRAM bios.

The systems runs, and it's faster than the old 66Mhz 486 CPU, but not by much. We have run
several of the available W95 based benchmarks against an original XL 466d2 and the upgraded
system, and a XL 466D2 system upgraded to a 100Mhz 486. The 100MHz 486 system was
the fastest, followed by the pentium upgrade system. I some of the test the original XL 466d2
system was FASTER than the upgraded pentium system !

Because of that, and the trouble we had with Intel (who blamed DEC), and DEC (who blamed
Intel), we removed the overdrive CPU, and re-installed the 486 CPU and cache, and reset the
Bios parameters. This whole adventure took 2 months because of the finger pointing, and DEC
engineering research.

We may have just seem the ugly side of this problem, and DEC has created a better solution that
gives a full speed upgraded overdrive pentium CPU with all CPU cache enabled, so if anyone
else has completed this upgrade, let me know.

M. Jerabek
multivac@hsq.com




