You might be right . . . the question from before is then "How can a 333
mghtz alpha run a little slower than a 200 mghtz pentium pro, even in
native?"

It might be a case of MS code being optimized for intel hardware.  About a
year ago PC Mag ran an article that compared the fastest pentium boxes
with the fastest NT PowerPC boxes.  The PowerPC boxes were a little faster
in non MS software (e.g. Photoshop), but were ~half as fast in MS software
(!).  This leads me to believe that there could be a 2X advantage in some
cases.  Thus it might actually be faster to run in FX!32 than in native,
in some cases (!!!).

This is not as bad as it sounds, because most Office software is not super
cpu intensive.  Word and Excel don't usually have to crunch that much. 
The real advantage for the alpha would be for codes like autocad, etc, and
yes, those are probably a lot faster.



