In article <56sr4f$7m4@leol.net-link.net>,
Todd C. Campbell <toddc@net-link.net> wrote:
>
>Could somebody please tell me what the memory modules 
>for the VS 3100 m30 are called?
>
   VAXstation 3100 Models (except the Model 76 which uses SIMMs) have memory
   daughter boards (MS42-xx) as follows:



  One adaptor         ================       4MB (-AB) or 12MB (-BA) board
                        ||




  Two adaptors        ====================   8MB (-KA) or 16MB (-CA) board
                        || ||
                        ||


                       ||
       =====================   4 MB on CPU board (one adaptor)

(1) You can hook up any of the memory boards 4, 8, 12 or 16 MB directly to
    the CPU board to get 8, 12, 16 and 20 MB total respectively,

(2) If you have one of the two-adaptor 8 or 16 MB boards hooked up to the
    CPU board, you can link an additional 4 or 12 MB board to its underside.
    That gets you to 12 or 24 MB total with the MS42-KA board; 20 or 32 MB
    total with the  MS42-CA board.  You cannot connect two of the two adaptor
    boards together.

  So the following configurations are possible:

   Total                         Configuration
   Memory                        (CPU board +)
     8 MB  (4+4)                 MS42-AB
    12 MB  (4+8)                 MS42-KA
    16 MB  (4+8+4) or (4+12)     MS42-AB + MS42-KA or MS42-BA
    20 MB  (4+16)                MS42-CA
    24 MB  (4+8+12) or (4+16+4)  MS42-KA + MS42-BA or MS42-CA + MS42-AB
    32 MB  (4+16+12)             MS42-CA + MS42-BA


> And what is available as far as upgrades.


  That all depends on what you have already.  If you only have one memory
board attached, you can add one of the other type.  If you already have two,
you'll have to swap one of them for the higher variant.

  If you need an 8 MB MS42-KA, I just swapped one of mine for a 16MB board
and I'm selling it for $135.

--
Vance Haemmerle
vance@alumni.caltech.edu
my VS 3100 page: http://condor.lpl.arizona.edu/~vance/toyvax.html




