In article <329229F1.31C7@dgii.com>, dougz@dgii.com says...
> H.W. Stockman wrote:

> So?  The Alpha clocks at up to 500MHz.  That's its design advantage.  
> It can be clocked very fast.  You will never see Intel catch-up on 
> this. Compare the fastest Alpha to the fastest Intel.

When I'm running graphics intensive apps - which by the very nature
mostly run in the graphics chip, and require all the host-to-board
bandwidth one can afford - CPU clock speed is not that fundamentally
important. I have indications that even at 200Mhz, many graphics
intensive environments will hog the PCI bus to a point that the CPU
will actually be waiting for the bus quite a lot. I'm not so eager
to get a 500Mhz CPU as I'm eager to get a 66Mhz AGP where I can move
data at both edges of the clock.

The first 200Mhz P6's we got were visibly slower than our 133Mhz P5's.
We found out that the reason was, there was a bug in the PCI bridge
chip that prevented the bus from bursting. Result: abysmal graphics
performance, which drags everything else down. Actually, I found that
I can turn off dual pipelining and branch prediction on a P5, and the
performance in a graphics environment won't degrade by more than 15%
or so; but if I swap my fancy graphics board for a lesser one, even a 
newbie can spot the slowdown.

There's much more to computer performance than just CPU megahertz.


Alberto.





