THE QUESTION:
------------

Can I use a 28.8 or higher modem with my older DEC notebook, or am I 
forever limited by the hardware to 14.4 bps?  If anyone can offer any info, 
I'd appreciate it.

THE BACKGROUND:
--------------

I have a DEC 425 SLC (486/25 16RAM).  I recently decided to upgrade the modem 
from a 14.4 v.32 DEC PCMCIA to a new USR Sportster 28.8/33.6 external to use 
with the computer's external serial port.  

Unfortunately, while using the USR, the computer limited connection speeds to 
19200 bps maximum; at connection speeds set higher than that, the USR 
immediately disconnects after negotiating with the modem at the other end 
(also a USR 28.8 Sportster).  In addition, even while connected at a reported 
19200 bps, the USR modem was effectively 3000-4000 bps slower during data 
transfer than the DEC PCMCIA modem I have been using.

I have been told several things about this problem:

	(a) the computer's UART chip is too slow to allow me to use an 
external 28.8 with the external COM port, and, 

	(b) the solution may be to get a 28.8 PCMCIA card modem since it 
communicates "directly" with the motherboard (this sounds fishy to me...).  
This theory attempts to explain why the 14.4 card modem offers effectively 
higher data through-put than an external modem connected at 19.2 bps.

Any suggestions?  Should I try a 28.8 card modem, or would I just be wasting 
time and money?

Thanks for any advice,

Darin Cosgrove



