[...]
> of word and excel that sped them up  .. . . are there
> any assembly calles stuck in critical places?

> PC magazine said that there was optimization.  One
> example was spell checking.  On the newer word it
> was done in real time, took almost no time.  The
> powerpc version had to slog through.

I'm interested in knowing, since this discussion
comes up a lot.  Can you get the PC Mag reference,
perhaps reproduce the paragraph for us here?

It would make sense to do some asm optimizations,
especially for the x86, which resists HLL optimization
(almost every C program I've written for x86 called
some asm functions).  But couldn't one do the same 
thing for RISC?  Or would that just bring us back
to the isue of RISC (supposedly) not needing as
many hand-tuned optimizations, because of the large
register set and symmetrical instructions, and good
compilers?

MS Word on a PPro does string searches much faster than
MS Word on a Pentium at a similar clock speed.  That
suggests to me that the searches were using x86 asm
string instructions, which were fast on the 386, slow
on the 486 and Pentium, and are once again fast on the
PPro due to an added hardware trick.  If MS had
truly optimized Word at the asm level, wouldn't they
have replaced the x86 string instructions with atomic
equivalents during the long dominance of the 486
and Pentium?

All speculation on my part of course, and I'm happy
to label it as such.



