In Article <E0z75z.A4v@world.std.com>
"Terry C. Shannon" <shannon@world.std.com> writes:
>Did anyone happen to attend Steve Zalewski's talk on OpenVMS OS Technical 
>Directions at DECUS Anaheim? T'was arguably the best presentation of the 
>week. It raises verrrrry interrrrresting implications for the future of 
>OpenVMS. 
>
>In a nutshell, Galaxies is a software architecture that will enable 
>clusters in a cabinet. Instead of building a box with, say, 32 CPUS (and 
>gee, I wonder who might be planning to do just that???) and running 'em as 
>a mongo SMP system, a Galaxies box might contain 32 CPUs partitioned into 
>8 4-way SMP nodes which are clustered together and which have node-private 
>memory and I/O as well as access to application and system shared memory.

Well, so what? It is pretty easy to do that now. Well, if dec will
still sell you a rack ;)

I can see that we need to look 2 ways. First is SMP chips. CPU speeds
are still increasing to hell, and DRAM isn't. For lots of $$$ and power
you can have huge fast cache, but the miss rate still kills you, and
there is a limit too the pain/bugosity you can engineer into a 21264
type chip to cover the latency. Soooo, if we cross an old PP with
a data-flow machine we can implement a `multi-CPU' on a chip. One
set of CPU logic, partitioned into functional units, just as it is
now, a cache on chip, and multiple register sets. The scheduling logic
would switch to a CPU that was not waiting for cache/memory. Note that
all the out of order tricks will still work within a CPU-stream, but
we add the advantage of another level of latency coverup.

So, 8 or 16 CPUs on a chip is possible. Even with one chip per board
it would be easy to exceed the max cluster size in a rack :)

The other way is much bigger clusters, but scaling problems there,
or a supra-cluster system that enables multiple clusters to
co-operate as if, to the end user most of the time, thay where one
system.

>Looks like WayCool stuff, providing that DEC management decides to make 
>the relatively modest investment in "productizing" the technology.

Modest?? I wonder about that. About DEC managment/marketing, no
doubts there though...

~Paul




