Close enough I suppose.

|On with the question...
|
|I do not usually get involved with DECNet but have had 
|reason to recently and am trying to understand if the 
|configuration I have encountered is legal.
|
|In the configuration there is a DECNet host which is 
|connected to two lan segments and is acting as a DECNet 
|router. The two interfaces on this host have the same DECNet 
|MAC address - this I believe is probably legal in the DECnet 
|world.

Legal and I believe mandatory - DECnetIV encodes the layer 3
address into the MAC physical address, in order to be able
to avoid having to use a layer 3->layer 2 address resolution
protocol.

|However, this customer also has a bridged network, and in 
|some circumstances it is possible for these two lan segments 
|to be bridged. Thus the bridges can see the same MAC address 
|on both of its ports.. not a good idea from the bridge
|point of view.

Nope.

|This is a greatly simplified picture of the network which 
|actually has >70 bridges within the network.
|
|________________________________
|      |                   |
|     DECNet Host          |
|      |                   |
|-------------          Bridge
|           |              | 
|          Bridge          | 
|           |              |
|        ------------------------

Not legal.

|Thus the question is if DECNet allows the same MAC address 
|on ports connected to two, does it assume that the network 
|would be engineered such that these two LAN segments would 
|not then be bridged. 

Yes - if running DECnetIV on multiple interfaces on a single
node, then it's assumed that the LANs to which those interfaces
are attached will NOT be hooked together at layer 2 or below.

In other words would the above 
|configuration be considered legal or illegal.

Illegal.

|Any feedback you be greatly appreciated.

Could replace those bridges with bridge-routers that
route DECnetIV and bridge all other protocols.  We
probably have a few such boxes we could sell 'em ... :-)

