<Catchup after long trip>
I'm not sure where all the numbers came from in this thread, but there was
clearly a lot of confusion, which I'll try to clear up.

BACKGROUND:
1. ~ 4X/3 : 4X / 3 years
	- Bits/DRAM
	- Bit density (& thus capacity) for small disks
	- Raw disk transfer rate (more-or-less, for large blocks)
    ==> 16X / 6 years

2. As Dave Olson noted, really big disk farms will go Fibre Channel:
	- 1 F.C. disk = ~ 8 MB/s raw transfer rate
	- 1 full-speed F.C = 100MB/s peak
	  = 70-80 MB/s sustained, i.e., bandwidth-dominated applications
	    Call this 8-10 disks to saturate the channel
	    = 1 tray (10 disks) in rack that can hold 10 trays.

3. 1 Origin2000 Module (assuming 195Mhz)
	- 4 node cards (1 node = Hub + 2 CPUs)
	- 2 XBOWs, each with 6 XIO ports to outside, and 2 XIOs to Hubs

Unlike a typical bus-based system, Origins have so many places you can make
bandwidth cuts that there is room for confusion. When I describe these,
I call the "sustained" I/O bandwidth of such a module 5 GB/s,
(= 2 XBOWs * 2 XIO-Hub * 1.25 GB/s "sustained" = 5 GBs).
that is, I make the cut between the XBOWs and the Hubs, since:
	(a) That number is independent of the particular choice of I/O
	    devices actually attached, node cards actually installed, etc.
	(b) That cut is actually wired into the midplane, unlike most other
	    things.

[As noted in some earlier posting, there is, in non-increasing order:
	(a) Peak (guaranteed never to achieve)
	(b) Sustained (really: peak-payload-bandwidth = peak minus obvious
		overhead, ignoring contention effects), but which most people
		call "sustained", even though it isn' .
		Guaranteed never to exceed.
	(c) Actual, which varies wildly, and is seldom a simple number.
		= Actual sustained.

In making comparions, (a) can be very misleading when comparing different
kinds of systems, for instance, when comparing multiplexed and split
address/data busses, (c) is difficult to express succinctly, and often impossible to get on competitors' systems, so I tend to use (b) for sanity.]

Now, the maximum (announced) Origin2000 is 128 CPUs = 16 modules.
5 GB/s * 16 modules = 80 GBs (sustained), as measured at the cut boundary
I mentioned.  The bisection bandwdith would be 20 GB/s.

4. Commentary
  Now, at this instant:
(a) No one has actually built & Q/Ad a configuration that could achieve that.
(b) It is difficult to configure peripherals that actually push hard on those
limits, now.
(c) The current node cards certainly do not have enough memory bandwidth
to source/sink all that data, i.e., the memory bandwidth is about 50% of the
I/O bandwidth.
	Someone is likely to complain that this makes no sense at all ...
	however, what they might not have understood is that CPUs may well
	choose to do uncached reads/writes to I/O devices, and this takes
	I/O bandwidth that does not necessarily deal with memory b/w.
	[There are other such cases, but the obvious one is graphics-driven-
	by-uncached-stores.]
(d) Somebody mentioned the fact that the bisection bandwdith is less than the
I/O bandwidth, and maybe that is a bottleneck.
	(a) There are workloads in which it might be: for example, suppose
	    that you had really fast networks & disks, so they weren't the
	    bottleneck.  Suppose you split the system in half (bisection),
	    and part A was using its half of the total I/O (40 GBs) to
	    read data and send it across the bisection to part B, which used
	    the other half of the total to write the data elsewhere.
	    In that case, the 20 GB/s bisection bandwidth would be a bottleneck.
	    This might sounds like a crazy thing to do, but of course, there
	    are applications (like video servers) that actually have this
	    I/O pattern ... except that any rational person would configure
	    the system to mix the input and output devices, not separate them.
	 (b) With any reasonable distribution of accesses,
	    there is plenty of bandwidth, as data isn't constrained to funnel
	    through one specific bisection.

5. Back to Origin #s.
	Suppose you could fill up a 128-CPU Origin with FC boards (you can't,
	but suppose, looking forward):
		16 modules
		12 XIOs/module = 192 XIOs
		2 FCs/ XIO = 384 FCs
		Each FC ~ 80 MBs, as described above, say 10 disks
		Total: 384*.08 GBs = 30 GB/s, 3840 disks, at say 5 GB each,
			(i.e., looking for bandwidth) = ~ 20 TB.
	(This may seem like craziness, but really, I've been in customer
	discussions, before the product was even announced, that went through
	this kind of arithmetic.  We've already sold *Challenge*-based
	data warehouses in the 1TB-2TB range, and I'm not even counting
	the applications that cannot be talked about.  16X/6 ...
	

5. Philosophy
	(1) CPus get updated often, but:
	(2) I/O infrastructure lasts a long time, certainly, at least
	    6 years, maybe more.  6 years = 16X capacity & raw transfer rate
	    off disk.  Put another way, if the current trends continue,
	    in 6 years, we ought to have single disks that will do >100MB/s raw.
	    If you get 100MBs/disk, 80 GB/s = "only" 800 disks, i.e., 8 racks.

	    If you "over-engineer" anything, over-engineer I/O,
	    because you will grow into it.  AT least, money can buy bandwidth;
	    as usual, latency is more difficult.
	
-- 
