Hey, we're only engineers.  :-)  We are way to early to even think about how 
licenses would work.

>         My take is the  following  (surmised  from my conversations with
>     VMS engineers).
> 
>         There is a basic scaling  problem  with SMP systems in that they
>     are not _totally_ symmetric.  Under VMS, the primary CPU handles all
>     the timer interrupts, the I/O _completion_ interrupts, and one other
>     item  I can't recall.  As a result, and depending on your work load,
>     there is a "knee" in the performance versus number  of  cpu's  curve
>     such  that  adding  additional  cpu's  gives  proportionately _less_
>     additional performance per cpu added.

What you say is all true, but I think the "as a result" is wrong.  There is 
certainly a knee, but the biggest reason for it is multiple CPUs contending 
for resources.  However, the primary CPU is not usually (ever?) the 
bottleneck.  The usual bottleneck is spinlocks.  The discussion about the 
primary CPU in the SMP talk was there more to give some background about why 
it used to be dangerous to use explicit affinity (you force your process 
onto a particular non-primary CPU and then do something that requires the 
primary) than it was to hype Galaxies.  The spinlock part of the talk was to 
hype Galaxies :-)

  In VMS, this occurs at around
>     4 cpu's for a "typical"  general  work  load (judging from the VUP's
>     marks  for SMP VAXen). 

I think this is a serious overgeneralization (as is the concept of VUPs).  
Also, I don't think you can necessarily compare VAX to Alpha for stuff like 
this.

In any case, though, there are lots of people running 12-CPU systems just 
fine.  It really depends heavily (perhaps more heavily than you implied) on 
the workload.  If lots of CPUs are doing computes with not much I/O or other 
OS interaction, you can get a lot of CPUs doing useful work while staying 
out of each other's ways.



