agtabby@aol.com wrote:

> It might be a case of MS code being optimized for intel hardware.  About a
> year ago PC Mag ran an article that compared the fastest pentium boxes
> with the fastest NT PowerPC boxes.  The PowerPC boxes were a little faster
> in non MS software (e.g. Photoshop), but were ~half as fast in MS software

There are vast differences between photoshop and
MS applications like Word and Excel.  An operation
like a graphics filter has extreme locality of reference,
and can run largely out of the registers.  A word
processor handles memory very differently, typically
has much worse locality of references, and favors
efficient memory access and string operations
over a large register set and multiple execution units.
Spreadsheets do a lot of processing of small tokens,
and are not very much like photoshop, either.

So when you compare Photoshop to "MS applications",
you are probably not comparing software by very different
companies, but rather very different paradigms.  If
your life revolves around photoshop, go for the machine
that gives you the biggest bang for the buck.  I have
personally been surprised by how important photoshop
seems to be in the world of computing (as judged by
the number of references to photoshop in this newsgroup).

Word and Excel are basically written in C.  It is up
to the compiler for the platform to do the optimization,
and you can bet DEC has a vested interest in having
the most efficient compilers applied to the task.  The
rumor of Intel optimization in Word and Excel is interesting,
but suggests that C is somehow biased toward the Intel
platform.



