Pudge <pudge@delphi.com> wrote:
>Too expensive

Current Alpha chips, yes. Forthcoming Alpha chips, maybe not. Note that 
the DEC/Mitsubishi 21164PC chip is expected to sell for around $425, 
placing it in the same league as an Intel Klamath. The forthcoming (first 
silicon is already out) Samsung Alpha implementation also will be cheap.

The issue for Alpha in the mainstream is applications availability. 
Digital Semiconductor's answer is the FX!32 code translator. FX!32 is 
slick technology, but DEC Semi may have Great Expectations nonetheless.

Note that FX!32 is designed to provide up to 70 percent native 
performance. It currently runs at ~50 percent native; your mileage may 
vary. In fact, I saw the editor of BackOffice magazine run the BackOffice 
benchnmark suite on a 200 MHz Pentium Pro system and on a 500 MHz Alpha 
system. The results were almost identical.

What this means is that DEC must dramatically increase Alpha performance 
or scale back its expectations for Alpha in the mainstream. If DEC's goal 
is to compete with Intel, Alpha must offer at least three times the native 
performance of Intel in order to enable translated apps to run at twice 
Intel speeds. (DEC realizes that it takes a twofold performance delta to 
convince customers to switch architectures.)

I really don't see this happening. But for customers who run technical 
apps (e.g. CAD, MCAD, MCAE, visual stuff, etc) on their workstations and 
who also want to run personal productivity apps, Alpha and FX!32 make good 
sense. The low-cost Alpha chips can only help the cause.

Terry Shannon
