From: makino@chianti.c.u-tokyo.ac.jp (Jun Makino)
Subject: Question on Alphastation PCI interface
Date: 1996/11/16
Message-ID: <MAKINO.96Nov16220107@muscat.c.u-tokyo.ac.jp>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 196906202
organization: College of Arts and Sciences, Univ. of Tokyo
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Hi all,

I have a question concerning the behavior of the PCI inteface of the
Alphastation 600. It seems the bandwidth of the Programmed I/O read is 
much lower than what is described in the document, while that of PIO
write operation is okay.

Does anybody know the reason why?

We developed a PCI interface card to connect some of our custom-made
hardware to Alphastations. Currently, we use the PIO operation for the
data transfer in both direction, since the performance data given in
the article "The Design and Verification of the AlphaStation 600
5-series Workstation" by John H. Zurawski, John E. Murray, and Paul J. 
Lemmonin, which appeared in DEC techincal journal Vol 7, No.1,
indicated that PIO read/write should give sufficient data transfer
speed for our purpose.

For the PIO write operation, the data transfer speed we got is
consistent with the data shown in the DTJ article. However, for PIO
read operation (consective read of 64-bit data in dense address
space), the performance we got was much lower than the data in the DTJ 
article.

The reason for the low read bandwidth is that the PCI interface of the 
Alphastation 600 does not generate the PCI read burst with more than 8 
bytes, while the numbers in the DTJ article clearly indicate that the
read burst should take place when possible. For PIO write, it
generates PCI burst write, as expected.

I would appreciate any information which might be related to this
difference.

Thanks

Jun Makino
University of Tokyo
http://grape.c.u-tokyo.ac.jp/~makino/index-e.html




