>What I would call "full" modem control - i.e. that RS232
>signaling necessary to talk properly to a modern modem -
>is 7 wires: ground, data (TX, RX), hardware flow control (CTS, RTS),
>and modem control (DTR, DSR.)

Close, but no cigar !

What is typically called "full" modem control for an asynchronous port
would also include ring (RI) and Carrier Detect (CD).  There is also a High
Speed (HS) signal which is probably optional.  HS used to signal 1200 vs
300 bps on a Bell 212A (Now you all know how old I really am !)

There are actually additional signal defined (such as a secondary channel)
but I have never seen them used on an async port.

It is also important to note that there is a proper sequence of when these
signals are activated that must be followed to be in true compliance of the
RS232 spec.  Unfortunately, almost no one in either the PC or Unix industry
does follow the specs (VAXen and, I suspect, Alphas running VMS hold to the
letter of the law.)

