Eric <ebush@sccsi.com> writes:

>Greetings:
[...]

>What I have concluded from this is that the case style does not matter,
>more the CPU board and backplane thats inside.  Is my theory correct?

Absolutely. The 11/44 system (or whatever) is defined by the CPU, not the 
case, or the peripherals. In fact, some systems were available with 
multiple mounting box styles for CPU boards alone.

The 11/44 CPU is several boards. From memory, you'll have : 

M7090 (dual height) - Console Interface
M7091, M7092 - Commercial Instruction Set Option
M7093 Floating Point Option 
M7094 Data Path (ALU + Registers)
M7095 Control (Microcode store, etc)
M7096 MultiFunction Module (Console Uarts, control, etc)
M7097 Cache Memory (was this an option? The machine can run without it)
M7098 Unibus (The main peripheral bus) interface.

The boards labelled as 'option' may not be present in your machine. 

If you have those boards in the right-hand backplane of the CPU box, then 
you have an 11/44


>I'm going to take the plunge and try and boot it for the 1st time
>tonight. If I should get lucky and have it come up without any faults

I'll probably get flamed for this like the last time, but I really don't 
recomend doing that. It's a lot better, particularly with an 'unknown' 
machine to bring the machine up a bit at a time starting with just the 
CPU power supply (I know, _for a fact_ that the H7144 supply can run 
unloaded). If the power supply is OK, then add the CPU boards and 1 
memory board and check that works. Then add peripherals one at a time 
until you have the whole machine working. 

>I'll still be a little outta luck (don't have any passwords for the
>system). If this thing is like a PDP8/E that I used at the same school I
>may get the prompt:
>load, run, start, etc: (shrug).  Wish me luck.

The 11/44 _may_ come up in Console-ODT (a machine code monitor that runs 
on an 8085 on one of the CPU boards) that lets you edit memory, run 
programs, etc. Or, it may have been set to auto-boot a particular device 
- probably an RK05. Do you have any manuals? If so, it's worth reading 
them _before_ you apply power for the first time.

Please feel free to contact me (either by e-mail or on Usenet) about 
this. I've worked on 11/44's and I've worked on RK05's, and know a few 
useful tricks.


