In article <3288DD91.1361@sccsi.com>, Eric <ebush@sccsi.com> wrote:
:Greetings:
:
:I rescued what I was lead to belive to be a 11/44 from my old high
:school. The system has 2 RK05 drives, an upright 1/2" tape unit, a
:cartridge tape unit on the front cabinet (just below the CPU), and a ton
:of the packs that are used in the RK05's.
:
:I bumped into a "DEC Archive" that had a jpg photo of a "11/23" that
:looks *just* like my system
:(http://www.msn.fullfeed.com/~cube/pdp11/pdp24sys.jpg).  The tag on the
:front of my box sports "11/44". Now I'm really confused.
:
:What I have concluded from this is that the case style does not matter,
:more the CPU board and backplane thats inside.  Is my theory correct?

	Well yes.
	No chassis that says 11/44 will have an 11/23 in it because an 11/23 
is a Qbus machine whereas the 11/44 is a Unibus machine.
	But you mention indirect 11/24. That is possible. The 11/44 did come 
with TU56 cartridge tapes. I can't remember whether the 11/24 did. Although I 
can't remember the M module numbers, a quick look at the CPU would tell: The 
11/44 requires several separate 6-wide boards to implement the CPU, using AMD 
2901 bit slice. You should see at least 4 AMD2901 ceramic 40pin packages. The 
PDP 11/24 was implemented using LSI, all on one board. I think there were 2 
CPU 40 pin chips plus an optional 3rd FPU chip.

		Cheers,
		Dan Ts'o
