(This has drifted way off topic, but I cannot resist the nostalgia :-) )

If I have disentagled all the quoting right, in article
<562bnd$708@nntp.ucs.ubc.ca>, Tim Shoppa <shoppa@alph02.triumf.ca> wrote:
>>>Ah - but Hugh was almost certainly using DZ-11 and DMF-32 serial cards.  
>>>Both are well-known performance degraders on Unibus VAX systems. ...

Then in article <562k19$lnb@panix2.panix.com> tls@panix.com (Thor Lancelot
Simon) said:
>>That's not right.  The DMF-32 is a pretty good VAX DMA serial card ...
>>However, the DMF hardware was... weird.

>>Most sites either used DZ's because they were dirt cheap, or DHU's (or other
>>vendors' DH-11 clones) because they offered much better port density.

Indeed, we used Able DH/DM clones (the DM portion provided the modem
control that the DH lacked).  These had 16 ports per board, rather
than the 8 on the DZ-11 and DMF-32.

The DMZ also had two more devices; if I recall correctly, these were
a parallel / line printer port and a synchronous serial port.

>>There was a 16-port? 32-port? relative of the DMF-32 called the DMZ-32
>>but they must have been really, really expensive since I never even
>>heard of anyone using them.

I suspect this was a 16-port board, then, because we had a clone
card that provided 48 ports.  The DMZ (or its clone) lacked the
parallel and synchronous ports.

The main problem with DZ-11s was that they required an interrupt
per character, even for output (typical serial port usage is
`trickles of input, torrents of output').  I believe there was no
input silo alarm (though one could, and 4BSD eventually did, do
polling on clock ticks instead).

>>DEC in its infinite wisdom of course inflicted the design of the DZ-11 on a
>>whole generation of its workstation customers by building a dual-DZ11 chip
>>and using it in preference to the at least not entirely useless 8530s used by
>>others.  Even worse, some of the things didn't have hardware flow control...

Neither, for that matter, does the Zilog 8530.  (It has a broken
half-implementation of hardware flow control instead.)  The 8530
also has only a two-character receive silo and a one-character
transmit buffer, and, like the DZ-11, requires an interrupt per
character.  (The lack of DMA is not all that serious, but a 16
to 64 character silo on each side would help tremendously.  When
the 8530 was designed, this was obviously a big deal in terms of
transistors, but these days... oh well.)

Finally, in article <564aip$squ@herald.concentric.net>, Daniel Ts'o
<dantso@cris.com> wrote:
>	As I vaguely recall, at least with Unix, even on DH-11's and DMF's  
>(forget about DZ's), the DMA buffer was quite small, I think from 8 to 60 
>bytes ...

The DH, DMF, and DMZ all used more or less the same DMA technique,
with a 16-bit counter, so would have been able to transfer up to
65536 bytes per interrupt.  4BSD at least, however, used something
called `clists' (a special block of memory that was mapped into
Unibus space once at startup, more or less), and clist memory was
broken into uniform blobs of 64 (really CBSIZE; it may have been
16 or 32 originally) bytes.  Four of those were used as a pointer
to the next cblock.  This is where the 60-characters-per-interrupt
limit came from.

The DH-11 had a programmable input silo alarm, so it was easy to
use efficiently for input as well (set the silo level as high as
you could tolerate in interrupt latency, and the timeout as high
as you could tolerate in absolute time latency, and away you go).
