ktcnslt@thenet.net (Tim Keating) writes:
|       256 GB of memory 
|       64 I/O interfaces with 192 I/O controllers (or 184 XIO and 24 PCI-64) 
|      128 3.5-inch Ultra-SCSI devices and 16 6.25-inch devices 
| 
|   Note: The 128 3.5" drive limitation.. Also They are limited to 64 I/O buses(most likely moderate
| speed PCI equiv 40-50MB/sec) buses into memory.. 

That's just for the drive bay's we provide "internally".  184 XIO is
(theoretically, not yet in practice) 736 ultra/wide scsi controllers,
each with a peak of 40MByte/sec, or just shy of peak theoretical
30GB/sec.  Of course, in reality if you were doing that many disks
(either capacity or throughput) you  would probably use something like
fibre channel, or serial hippi, not ultra scsi.

Each of those 64 i/o interfaces are a crossbar chip, with 3 XIO
slots.

Oh, and by the way, that's basicly one 64bit 33MHz PCI bus per board,
with 64 bit PCI scsi controllers, and we definitely have moved
200MB/sec through them sustained, so PCI isn't likely to be your
limitation, although with small enough i/os it might be.
