Romel Rausa Llarena <rrl@wwnet.com> wrote in article
<32836A43.70F2@wwnet.com>...
> Is it possible to assign a TCP/IP address dynamically to a Windows NT
> client connected to a network running Pathworks? If so what version of
> Pathworks and what products will I need.
> 
> Thanks!
>

As far as I'm aware, there's nothing in PATHWORKS that'd be a problem
with DHCP.

PATHWORKS licensing bits rely on the station address (which is either
the hardware address from the network adapter, or the DECnet address,
if the system runs DECnet).

DHCP won't change the station address.

About PATHWORKS on Windows NT: The current release + patches (which
you really should get ;-) works only on Windows NT 3.51. The next
release (due out early next year - unless things have changed) will
support Windows NT 4.0 (it will then be a single kit for Windows 95
and Windows NT - i.e., many of the features on the DOS/Windows 3.x
and Windows 95 versions of PATHWORKS will finally be available also
on Windows NT). 

See 

   http://www.digital.com/info/pathworks

for more.

If you only need the PATHWORKS client licensing bits on Windows NT,
then you can copy a licensing kit from 

   ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/PCI
 
Look for files starting with FPA* (there's also an index file with
descriptions in the directory).

Also, PATHWORKS issues are probably best discussed on

   vmsnet.networks.desktop.pathworks

regards,
...petri (petri.backstrom@icl.fi)
