RTEM is an RT-11 emulator that runs on RSX.  It also runs on VAX under 
VAX-11/RSX.  On modern VAXen without PDP-11 hardware emulation, VAX-11/RSX
uses a software emulator.  As previously mentioned, the emulator design can
go considerably faster if you give it enough memory.  It is also true as
previously mentioned that the PDP-11 hardware emulation stayed about the same
speed on later VAXen (8600) as on earlier VAXen (780).  However, the native
speed of your VAX counts for a lot: most applications spend a lot of time
running system code, and under VAX-11/RSX, that runs in VAX native mode.  The
PDP-11 hardware emulator on the original 780 runs about as fast as a PDP-11/23.
However, I've experienced throughput more similar to an 11/44 on a VAX-780,
running both RSX programs under VAX-11/RSX and RT-11 programs under RTEM under
VAX-11/RSX.  I recall that the VAX 8800 (which lacks PDP-11 hardware emulation)
became the fastest PDP-11 when it was released, albeit through software 
emulation.  (The PDP-11/84 was then the top "real" PDP-11 at the time, so I 
don't know how the 8800 compares the the 11/94 & 11/93.  But there are much 
faster VAXen now than the 8800...)

As for RTEM, I've used it on both RSX and VAX VMS and it works fine.  The only
drawbacks are:

	It was retired at RT-11 V5.4, while RT-11 went on to V5.6.
	You only get the FB monitor, no XM or SJ or such.

Since RTEM is retired, you might check with DECUS to see if it is available
on any of the DECUS tapes.  Failing that, check with Mentec.  On VAX, you
also need VAX-11/RSX.  I don't know if that was sold to Mentec or not, but
I imagine it was.

Another company, whose name escapes me at the moment (S & H Software?), made a
nice multi-user RT-11 emulator called TSX+ that runs on top of RT-11 on a 
PDP-11 (actually, RT-11 boots first then TSX+ takes over, giving you a multiuser 
environment).  I don't know if TSX+ runs on VAX in any form.  TSX+ is not a 
Digital product, and so would not  have been part of Digital's sale of the 
PDP-11 business to Mentec.  But the  folks at Mentec may have more current info 
about it.

/Jack

Opinions are mine and not necessarily those of Oracle Corporation.

-- 
