Zalman Stern wrote:
> 
> Tony Griffiths (tonyg@OntheNet.com.au) wrote:
> [Stuff about FX!32 and "whose bug is it?"]
> 
> I believe FX!32 is intended to provide perfect emulation of the x86
> execution environment. A such, if a program behaves a certain way on Intel
> x86 implementations and doesn't behave that way on FX!32, then it is an
> FX!32 bug. (As Jerry Leichter said, its just like an x86 clone vendor, only
> in software rather than silicon.)
>
Ah but FX!32 is _NOT_ an x86 emulator, it's a binary (JIT?) translator.

Now consider the following TRUE example of a "latent" bug-

Proc A calls Proc B with certain args.  B executes and returns.  Proc A
then calls Proc C with SAME args _AND_ the local variables in C are
declared in the same order as B with a pointer to a structure being the
first local var.  Proc B properly computes this pointer from the input
args, but proc C doesn't.  However, proc C executes 'correctly' on
Digital Unix for many years because the stack location that the pointer
variable is allocated to just happens to have the pointer to the correct
structure in it, put there by Proc B.

Now the code is ported to VMS on Alpha.  The VMS C compiler is more
agressive in stack usage (slightly different compile time switches and
defaults are used) and now the memory location is different or possibly
re-used by another variable in a different scope.  The "working" code
falls in a heap and the system with it given that this is networking
code running in kernel mode!

This is the sort of problem I am referring to.  A true x86 emulator
might allow code like this to continue operating but an on-the-fly port
which is what FX!32 does cannot guarantee that buggy code will continue
to operate.  Whose fault is it in this situation?  FX!32 for not
providing the identical operating environment or the ISV's code which
has the latent bug?

How many other "mission critical" apps are out there with code paths
that have not been properly debugged and tested, just waiting for their
day in the limelight (or CNN news as the case may be ;-) ).  I would
suggest quite a few.

Tony



