> 
>> I believe FX!32 is intended to provide perfect emulation of the x86
>> execution environment. A such, if a program behaves a certain way on Intel
>> x86 implementations and doesn't behave that way on FX!32, then it is an
>> FX!32 bug. (As Jerry Leichter said, its just like an x86 clone vendor, only
>> in software rather than silicon.)

As an erstwhile developer on FX!32 I can tell you that we very much
embraced the position described above.

>> 
>> I get te impression CPU emulation is not really where the bugs occur. (Its
>> still a difficult problem for performance reasons, but emulation software
>> can be made incredibly reliable.) The bugs are in all the other services and
>> device drivers,
>
>Yes, if the program needs a dongle we have lost already.

Not so!  FX!32 has run many applications that required dongles.

>Let's hope the alpha has a true clone of the pc line printer port.

It does.

>Some dongles even provide executable code that dynamically changes
>from use to use and that's really hard to translate.

FX!32 uses translation to achieve speed when it know that doing so is
correct. Code that is not is not part of the disk image seen by the
offline translator cannot possibly get translated. This case covers
runtime code generation, stack-based thunks, etc. Another issue (which
FX!32 handles correctly) is truly self-modifying code. Here the code
gets translated offline but the translation cannot be used because it
no longer represents a valid translation of the x86 instruction text.

