In article <3283962F.11D397D9@triton.com>, Jay Olson <jjo@triton.com> writes:
|I am looking for a DECserver to connect a few consoles and a modem. What
|is the difference between DTR/DSR modem control (as on the DECserver
|90TL and 90M) and "full modem control" (as on the DECserver 700)? The
|modem will be used only for outgoing calls (with possible callback).

What I would call "full" modem control - i.e. that RS232
signalling necessary to talk properly to a modern modem -
is 7 wires: ground, data (TX, RX), hardware flow control (CTS, RTS),
and modem control (DTR, DSR.)

I think that (some?) DECserver 90's only give you 5 wires:
GND, TX, RX, DTR, DSR.  These means that they CAN handle
modems, but can't properly flow control them - which means
that you shouldn't run modems with compression on (speed
mismatch between line and DTE side.)  5-wire serial ports
are no good for running, say, a 28.8Kbps line with a 115.2
(or even 38.4) Kbps DTE.

If speed is no big deal to you, say, if you're happy with
9600 bps, then, sure, use 5-wire serial ports.  I dialout
at 9600 all the time (thru a 3-wire serial port), and that's 
good enough for me. 

Aaron



