[ ... ]

The DMZ was slicker than snot - we had hundreds of ports worth
of them in use at th UofAz for many years.  The neat thing about
them was that they TDM'd 24 RS232 channels over a T1 - so you 
could locate the distribution panel (which was actually a 
combination TDM mux and T1 line driver) on the other side of a T1.  

Even slicker, with the late, great Linkabit IDX data PBX, which also
TDM'd 24 RS232s over T1, you could run a T1 line straight from
the Unibus into the PBX, giving you a "data trunk line."  Or you
could take two of the distribution panels and put them back to back
to TDM 24 data channels over a local 4-wire line.

Note that the 24 channels in a T1 is the same scheme as is used in
POTS - each RS232 DTE got analog-sampled and sent as a 64Kb DS0
channel.  Which meant, if going thru a PBX, the PBX didn't need to
know what the baud rates were on the DTEs on either end of the
circuit - this scheme just delivered an analog pattern between the
DTEs, and as long as the DTEs were in agreement (up to 19.2Kbps),
they'd talk just fine.

Of course, packet switching clobbered data circuit switching (never
mind ISDN), so all this stuff has pretty fallen into the dustbin
of history ... ah well.

Cheers,

Aaron



