:That's not right.  The DMF-32 is a pretty good VAX DMA serial card, which had 
:reputation of being somewhat better than the DHU-11, the available Unibus DMA
:serial card.  However, the DMF hardware was... weird.
:
:Most sites either used DZ's because they were dirt cheap, or DHU's (or other
:vendors' DH-11 clones) because they offered much better port density.  There
:was a 16-port? 32-port? relative of the DMF-32 called the DMZ-32 but they 
must
:have been really, really expensive since I never even heard of anyone using
:them.
:
:But yeah, the DZ-11 was a dog and there are *still* far too many of them out
:there -- I have six current-loop DZ-11s myself, if anyone wants some. :-)
:
:DEC in its infinite wisdom of course inflicted the design of the DZ-11 on a
:whole generation of its workstation customers by building a dual-DZ11 chip
:and using it in preference to the at least not entirely useless 8530s used by
:others.  Even worse, some of the things didn't have hardware flow control...
:<thwonk>. 
:

	As I vaguely recall, at least with Unix, even on DH-11's and DMF's  
(forget about DZ's), the DMA buffer was quite small, I think from 8 to 60 
bytes, whereas those Ethernet packets can be 1500bytes.

