| [Concerning using binary translation to run x86 code on an Alpha]
| Anybody who wants to market binary recompilation as a compatibility
| solution would be well advaised to make sure that their product works
| just like the real thing, and that includes tolerating "buggy" code
| that happens to run on the real thing.

One could make *exactly* the same argument for a non-Intel implementa-
tion of the x86 chip set.  And, in fact, people did.  The premise of
Intel's "Intel Inside" advertising is that only an Intel processor can
*really* make a PC - i.e., some of your stuff may not work on a clone.

Nevertheless, third-party x86 clones are doing quite well in the market-
place now - well enough that if PC software won't run on them, the
public sees the responsibility for fixing things as belong to the
software maker, not the clone chip maker.

Hell, you could make the same argument for new versions of *Intel's*
chips!  If some software runs on 486's but not on Pentium's, is Intel
blamed?  If 16-bit software doesn't speed up on Pentium Pro's, what's
the reaction from the trade rags:  Well, Intel didn't read the market
quite right, but the transition to 32-bit software is on its way.

Ultimately, this all comes down to market perceptions.  There is *no*
rational basis for distinguishing, in principle, between new Intel
versions of the x86 ISA, clone chip versions, binary translators, and
x86 emulators.  That's not to say that people won't make those
distinctions!  And you can be sure that vendors of x86-chip-based
hardware will do their best to insinuate that, well, FX!32 isn't
*really* a Pentium....

How this will come out is up in the air.  (Unfortunately, given DEC's
past history of absolutely *dreadful* marketing, especially when it
comes to setting expectations - SunOS and Solaris and AUX and so on are
Unix, Ultrix and OSF/1 are "DEC's Unix-like operating systems" - I'm not
sanguine that the results will be favorable for FX!32.)

							-- Jerry



