> >|   In any event, the original topic (moving x86 NT apps to Alpha)... would
> >|you then suggest that such transitions are <a> easier or <b> more
> >|difficult than the example I provided (MacOS 68k->PPC)?
> 
> Don't know about MacOS but Tuesday I compiled some code (a couple
> hundred source files) that runs fine on x86 NT on an Alpha NT box and
> it again ran fine (actually, even better because it was a good deal
> faster).  No code modification was required, just a recompile.  So
> that was easy, however, a coworker has some driver code that works
> fine on the x86 NT and compiles fine on the Alpha NT but doesn't work
> on the Alpha NT.  The driver fails when trying to initialize the PCI
> bus data structures.  It is fixable but it means that it is a little
> different from the x86 stuff but what do you expect when you're
> writing hardware drivers?

Well my experience is that "Oils ain't oils", or in my case one Alpha
Workstation is not necessarily equal to another Alpha W/station even
when both have PCI buses.

When writing a PCI sync card driver, I noticed that the OS (Unix)
supported an I/O macro {READ,WRITE}_IO_D{8,16,32,64} so I tried the D64
variant and it worked, even though the machine on which I was writing
the driver had only 32-bit PCI.  Well it all came unstuck when DEC
released another machine with 64-bit PCI and someone on the other side
of the world (fortunately in the test lab in Mass or N.H.) plugged the
32-bit card into the 64-bit slot.  I had "great fun" debugging this from
16,000 Km away.  NOT!

So the moral of the story is =>  Don't write device driver for a
living...  You'll go mad!!!

Code that doesn't play with the guts of the machine and is well written
has a reasonable chance of working cross platform, even kernel code. 
Code that play with the hardware almost certainly breaks even on the
same architecture after the h/w engineers have done their bit to drive
us s/w people into a state of alcoholic oblivion.

