Tony Griffiths (tonyg@OntheNet.com.au) wrote:
[Stuff about FX!32 and "whose bug is it?"]

I believe FX!32 is intended to provide perfect emulation of the x86
execution environment. A such, if a program behaves a certain way on Intel
x86 implementations and doesn't behave that way on FX!32, then it is an
FX!32 bug. (As Jerry Leichter said, its just like an x86 clone vendor, only
in software rather than silicon.)

I get te impression CPU emulation is not really where the bugs occur. (Its
still a difficult problem for performance reasons, but emulation software
can be made incredibly reliable.) The bugs are in all the other services and
device drivers, etc. one needs to get working "just right" to run real
program. As in often one is reimplementing a fair it of system software to
make the emulator run. This is perhaps abit less of a problem for Alpha NT
'cause the Alpha version intheory uses the same code on both
platforms. However as with any port, the two versions are not guaranteed to
be exactly alike.

As an example, the product I work ondepends heavily on underlying video
capture/compression/decompression/playbcak subsystems in the OS. No amount
of CPU virtualization underneath our app is going to eliminate that realm
of problems.

