My impression was that RTEM, which originally ran under RSX, runs under
VAX/RSX in VMS.

There were a number of old RT emulators way back when which were basically
concocted by replacing RT11 drivers with $qio calls and some hacking
in low memory to save/restore the $DSW around calls and to keep the
low mem pointers RT used safe; these were in addresses below 64 as
I recall. They had been built using real RT11 sources in which low
level handling had been replaced. The drivers were special in that
they were original RT11 drivers, gutted and with $qio replacing the
actual hardware tweaking, and using ASTs (knowing there were an extra
4 words on the stack) to fake interrupts. Once you figured out the
stack format in an RSX AST, a bit of tweaking to make it look like you
had an interrupt was simple. The last one of these I saw was a V2C
emulator; it even kept the RT11 clock ticking. In fact, running
under RSX11D it would work with most RT sj monitor apps, but you needed
a real RT11 file structured disk with the utilities to use it. (The
lack of anything suitable was one reason I wrote an RSX11D virtual disk
and a serial driver that could munge single char $qio into file operations
so printer output could be spooled, in fact, in the mid-70s.)

I imagine the DEC product did something similar. At present you might
be well advised to write Mentec about this. (They are at mentec.com.)

The other route to running RT, if you have an RT system disk somewhere,
might be to pick up one of the pdp11 software emulators; these are
claimed to run any pdp11 OS and some may be buildable on alpha.
glenn everhart
DECUS RSX Tapecopy Coordinator emeritus



