Jim,

This has nothing to do with the Celebris.  This is a "feature" of DOS,
Windows 95, OS/2, and nearly any other operating system that needs to
keep some DOS compatibility.  Nearly every system BIOS is structured
as follows.

The order of assigning drive letters by DOS (neglecting multiple
partitions per drive, which is even still more confusing) is master
IDE drive connected to primary IDE controller, slave IDE drive
connected to primary IDE controller, hard disk with SCSI ID 0, hard
disk with SCSI ID 1, disks & disk-like devices via device drivers.

What to do about it?

1. I don't know for sure, but POSSIBLY an add-in EIDE card configured
as a SECONDARY IDE controller, supported by a device driver would do
the trick.  No guarantee here.  And no recommended brand of card.

2. Stick with SCSI.  Period.  Having made the investment in a system
with decent SCSI capabilities, use them.  SCSI is far more functional
and faster than IDE, subject to fewer quirks because it has been thru
a far more rigorous standards process than IDE, which has evolved
haphazardly.  (I know this means flames from folks who like IDE.)

...Ben Myers 


