nntp-posting-user: grunwald



The big problem is supporting multiple ISA's isn't the distribution
(that's what CD's are for) or compilation process, it's the product
testing and regression testing.

Some time ago, Microsoft bought the technology of a company called
OmniWare. Omniware (partly founded by Steve Lucco, formerly at CMU,
graduated from Berkeley) marketed a compiler, virtual instruction set
and machine translator for a "virtual computer".

This virtual computer is much like the JavaVM, but much easier to
translate to modern ISA's, and results in much faster code. In many
ways, this is similar to the "ANDF" system promoted by OSF that never
seemed to take off.

Anyway, imagine defining the system interface as something where
programs based on a system like OmniVM can be interpreted or
translated. Suddenly, your software vendors only need to test a single
platform (the virtual platform) someone else (microsoft?) needs to
insure that the virtual->physical binding is consistent across
systems.

You could even squirt the programs from one machine to another, even
in a heterogenous network.

Given this, you only need to distribute a single binary image and
allow it to be bound to the final system. This was the goal for ANDF,
but since ANDF was targeted for Unix-like systems, and two UNIX
vendors would *never* agree on anything that might make UNIX
successful, it didn't work. But microsoft can force it to work.


-- 
Dirk Grunwald                 Assoc. Prof, Univ. of Colorado at Boulder



