reply-to: alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com (Alan Rollow - Dr. File System's Home for Wayward Inodes.)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec



In article <bland-041196154444@141.214.13.251>, bland@umich.edu (Peyton Bland) writes:
>
>  class      x0022  DEC SIM
>  subsystem  x0000  Disk
>
>I have seen this for both disks and tape.  What does "DEC SIM" refer to? 
>Sometimes I see SIM94 (class x0020).  In general, where can this type of
>info be found?

Both of those are messages coming from the SCSI CAM driver subsystem.  Uerf
is rather brain-damaged about the what is important when it comes to printing
SCSI CAM errors, so the default listing only says that there was an error
and what device caused it.  In some cases the driver will generate its
own messages, so an entry won't be associated with any particular device;
the driver scheduling a user requested bus reset.

The terms SIM and SIM94 may be described in the guide for writing SCSI
CAM device drivers.  The include files may also offer clues about what
some of the data structure names mean.  These are in /usr/include/io/cam.

To find out what really happened, use the "-o full" option of uerf
to get the full listing.  Uerf does a fair job of translating the
SCSI information to a minimally useful error message.  But, to find
out in detail what want wrong with a device you have to get the device
documentation and look up the meaning of the Additional Sense Code (ASC)
and Additional Sense Code Qualifier (ASCQ) that are in the uerf listing.

Many driver generated messages include the routine name that generated
the message; cdisk or ctape names.  These might be documented in the
driver documentation.

>Thanks,
>Peyton
