I'll jump in here and point out that DEC RXV21's are only 18-bit
when doing DMA.  (When used in Programmed I/O RX01 mode, it doesn't
matter whether they are 18 or 22 bit.)  Sigma, I believe, made
a version which was 22-bit, but you needed a special driver in
order to use the extra functionality.  If you have a driver which
uses 22-bit functionality, you should specify where the driver is from
if you want to make sure a 22-bit RXV21 compatible will work with it.
22-bit RT-11 drivers for the Sigma 22-bit extension are available by
anonymous from

  sunsite.unc.edu

in the directory

  /pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/rt/decus/11s109/22bit_dev

The latest versions of all
DEC OS's (as well as 2.11BSD) all work fine if you drop a 18-bit
RXV21 into a system with more than 128 kWords of memory.  They all
allocate a buffer in low memory through which accesses are buffered.  As 
I recall, there was at least one version of RSX-11M (v4.0? v4.1?) which didn't
work on Q-bus systems with RXV21's if you had more than 128 kWords.
It didn't matter whether it was an 18-bit or a third-party 22-bit
RXV21; the first attempt to read into memory past 128 kWords corrupted
your system.

