Rob Barris wrote:

>    In any event, the original topic (moving x86 NT apps to Alpha)... would
> you then suggest that such transitions are <a> easier or <b> more
> difficult than the example I provided (MacOS 68k->PPC)?
>
Getting the code to (a) compile without error, and then (b) link is only
a SMALL part of a porting exercise.  Phase (c) is test, test some more,
and when that is finished test again!!!  It is this part of the exercise
when the big $$$s are introduced and which discourages
multi-architecture product releases.

An ISV that gets a bug report on an Alpha for a product that has been
FX!32'd can simply point the finger at DEC and say it is their problem
because the same bug doesn't appear in the Intel binary.  While this is
a legitimate tactic it does not necessarily mean that FX!32 has a bug
but could in fact actually be a "real" coding bug that only appears on a
different architecture due to differences in register/stack usage,
parameter passing, and the like.  In this case, it is likely that the
DEC software engineers will spend the time determining the problem and
simply tell the ISV that "your code is wrong at line <n> in module <y>,
which suits the ISV just fine!  ;-)

For the more common apps, it is DEC that will wear the testing cost.

Talking to one of our support people who owns a Power Mac, I would
suggest that Apple has NOT had a pleasant time porting their software to
this new architecture.

