We've been quite happy with our DEC Alpha machines (of which
our group now owns six single-CPU boxes, and has a ~65% share
of two four-CPU AlphaServer 2100 systems (one 4/200, and one
5/250)).

The compilers seem to have astoundingly good optimizers, and
we've found very few bugs.  Can't comment on C++, though, as
until recently it wasn't covered under CSLG, so the C++ gang
would use g++.

The only caution (and I may be stating the obvious) is that
when you say you're "impressed by the speed of the new alpha",
are you referring to the published performance data, or by a
trial run on your own application?  In our group, we usually
find that, given a set of systems from different vendors, the
relative performance for our applications doesn't necessarily
correlate well with, say, the relative SPECfp ratings.  So we
always insist on a couple hours of benchmarking time before
buying anything.

Ever since early 1993, when the first Alpha systems started to
ship, DEC has won every sale in our group (previously we went
with Sun and SGI).  Unfortunately, the next time around we may
end up buying from Sun, or possibly SGI; based on some sample
quotes from the three vendors for a system in the 120 SPECfp95
range with 2 Gbyte memory, Sun is the clear leader, while SGI
lags a bit behind, but DEC isn't even in the running.

