Jeff M. Horsager (horsager@accessone.com) wrote:
: On power up [of my 11/03] I get and '@' prompt on my terminal.


You are talking to "console ODT". The commands are
<address>   specify an octal memory location (followed by /)
/           print contents of a memory location (in octal)
<value>     store the value in an open memory location (followed by CR or LF)
CR          close an open loc
LF          close an open loc, open next loc
$ or R      open a processor register
RS or $S    open the processor status word
<address>G  start program execution at address
P           resume program execution (at the addr it was halted)

Typically you either enter a short bootstrap pgm (in octal) into memory
and then start it up. Such a pgm would read an OS from disk, for example.
Some systems have bootstrap pgms in ROM, which can be started from ODT.

