>>> "rbarris" == Rob Barris <rbarris@quicksilver.com> writes:

rbarris> In article <vwjzq11afnt.fsf@osfb.aber.ac.uk>, pcg@aber.ac.uk
rbarris> (Piercarlo Grandi) wrote:

piercarl> However there is a terrible marketing trap in successful
piercarl> emulation: that it essentially kills any incentive for
piercarl> application developers to target the native environment. OS/2
piercarl> is in many ways indeed a better Windows than Windows; so it is
piercarl> basically pointless, at least in marketing/volume terms, to
piercarl> develop for OS/2 itself.

rbarris> One thing through, since Win NT also runs natively on Alpha, it
rbarris> is supposed to be very straightforward for an app developer to
rbarris> go native (since the API's are the same) unlike the OS/2 vs
rbarris> Windows case.

Well, an *architecture* change is a rule a non trivial business; it
seems that a lot of apps are coded in a way that is rather dependent on
the x86 architecture. Even more problematic in the particular case is
that the ALPHA is really a 64 bit one, and the x86 one is not. Unless an
application is carefully coded to be word length independent, a lot of
problems can happen.

But this is a discussion of the *costs* of porting. My point was that
whatever the costs (which include porting and most importantly *support*
costs, which are several times larger), there is little marketing reason
to do it, because:

rbarris> This might only appeal to developers that have CPU intensive
rbarris> apps - but it seems clear that non-CPU-intensive apps probably
rbarris> don't need to be compiled native anyway.

Indeed -- and therefore there will be no reason to port them the native
architecture. Since there will be few native architecture ports, the
native architecture will not have the unique selling point of being
itself, and will only succeed as far as it manages to be good at trying
to be something else.

rbarris> It is a similar situation to MacOS on PowerPC - switching CPU
rbarris> architectures (assuming your code is in a high level language)
rbarris> is a walk in the park since the API's didn't change.

Well, not so easy. It was also an architecture change, and not entirely
painless, to say the least.


All in all, only if FX!32 is _really_ competitive at making the ALPHA be
a Pentium will ALPHAs become popular; and only when there will be lots
and lots of ALPHAs that pretend to be Pentiums developers will have any
volume-related incentive to target the native architecture to boast of
that 2x increase in speed.

This by and large has not happened with the OS/2 case, in part because
the speed differential between OS/2 and WIN-OS/2 apps is imperceptible,
and in part because Microsoft has steadfastly avoided to port any of its
best selling apps to OS/2, while it is still contractually committed to
port them to the Mac, which has more or less equivalent numbers.

Then of course they might well have a good incentive to port them to
their own Windows NT on a non Intel CPU: this would allow them to
commoditize the CPU market, and capture a large part of the rent that
currently accrues to Intel, and redefine the ``wintel'' platform as
the winnt platform.

The amount of money and patience Microsoft have invested in supporting,
if only at the OS level, architectures like the MIPS (and ALPHA and
PowerPC and SPARC) that are nearly irrelevant as to numbers show that
they dream about this.

I'll start being really impressed when one will be able to buy (for most
probably Microsoft will have already developed it internally) a native
port of Microsoft Office for ALPHA (or any non Intel Windows NT
platform), and it will be sold in some volume.



