Patrick Chase wrote:
> 
> In article <327FEF6E.783A@OntheNet.com.au>, Tony Griffiths <tonyg@OntheNet.com.au> writes:
> |> An ISV that gets a bug report on an Alpha for a product that has been
> |> FX!32'd can simply point the finger at DEC and say it is their problem
> |> because the same bug doesn't appear in the Intel binary.  While this is
> |> a legitimate tactic it does not necessarily mean that FX!32 has a bug
> |> but could in fact actually be a "real" coding bug that only appears on a
> |> different architecture due to differences in register/stack usage,
> |> parameter passing, and the like.  In this case, it is likely that the
> |> DEC software engineers will spend the time determining the problem and
> |> simply tell the ISV that "your code is wrong at line <n> in module <y>,
> |> which suits the ISV just fine!  ;-)
> 
> Yeah, right!
> 
> Aren't you presupposing that DEC will have access to ISV source code
> in order for this to happen?

No!  I've "debugged" code for which I had only the binaries and patched
it too!  ;-)

> Do you really think that Micro$oft (or

Well DEC has a cross-licence agreement with MS and DOES have access to
the sources as far as I am aware.

> anybody else, for that matter) will simply hand over their crown
> jewels in this fashion?

No.  This is where DEC (or anyone else doing binary translation) will
have to spend the dollars on a test lab staffed with competent
engineers.  The most common packages would be tested in this lab, and if
a problem appears the cause would be determined.  If it is 'buggy' code,
then DEC will have to try and convince the ISV (or MS) to change the
code and regenerate the original x86 binary.  The only other solution is
to patch the x86 binary and feed it through the translator again.  This
is NOT impossible but is time-consuming and costly!

> Also, given the strange bu immutable tendency
> for bug fixes to induce other bugs, do you believe that the ISVs
> will act on suggestions from another company unless they have an
> immediate, bottom-line reason to do so?
>

Yes and no...  some will and some won't.  It depends on the $$$s
involved.  The ISV is making money selling their x86 product on an Alpha
box and it is not always a wise marketing ploy to say "Shove your
Alphas, install a Pentium instead!" to the end user (who might be a very
large company).  The reason for buying the Alphas may involve other
factors such as FP performance what Alpha beat the pants off Intel cpus.
 
> Anybody who wants to market binary recompilation as a compatibility
> solution would be well advaised to make sure that their product works
> just like the real thing, and that includes tolerating "buggy" code that
> happens to run on the real thing.

This is the IDEAL but often reality raises it's ugly head.  A binary
translator is really only a compiler/code generator with a different
input stream.  Making it do different things for different apps
auto-magically is very difficult.  And lets face facts, there is a LOT
of buggy code out there that 'works' on a particular platform with a
particular compiler/linker combination.  It may or may not break in the
future on this same platform and there are certainly no guarantees about
ports to other platforms which is what a binary translation is!

> 
