We have a DEC Printserver17 (16Mb RAM) on our decnet/pathworks/MicroVax
3100-80 network and get a lot of problems printing via postcript from
P.C's on the network.
What usually happens is a job is submitted which prints but the printer
'sticks' in processing state (a look at the print queue reveals the job
as state STALLED) despite the fact the print will have completely
printed and no error message sheets printing.
Often, especially if a few copies of a few-page document are sent to
print the printer will print the first couple of copies and then fail
with an eon-of-job flushing error (should've made a note of the exact
error but I hadn't planned on posting today).
The obvious thing is a lack of memory (printing a font sample fails
after about 20 fonts (lack of memory) but reveals about 450k of free
memory) - is this correct for a 16Mb PrintServer17 ??? We have ordered a
further 16Mb, but tests reveal that even if a job prints successfully it
leaves about a 20k footprint in memory - which eventually means even
simple jobs fail and the printer needs to be rebooted (which at 15
minutes each time can get annoying) so it looks like the extra memory
will simply delay the failure to print. We presume that the printer has
been set up incorrectly - can someone confirm this and possibly suggest
some solutions ? A quick try with DEC support didn't get very far,
either the printer was probably to blame, the network cabling or the
DecNet setup depending on which department pass-the-buck. We don't want
to call out an engineer for what could be a simple set-up problem.
-- 
Nick Ferrar



