>>> "neideck" == Burkhard Neidecker-Lutz <neideck@kar.dec.com> writes:

neideck> In article <Dzyt5D.9Fs@rockyd.rockefeller.edu> Dan Ts'o
neideck> <dan@dnn.rockefeller.edu> writes:

dan> OK, so then what is the relative performance running Intel code
dan> between the Intel CPU and the Alpha running FX!32 ?

neideck> While a lot depends on the particular application, performance
neideck> of a 433 Mhz 21164A ranges from a 150 Mhz Pentium to beyond a
neideck> 200 Mhz Pentium Pro in things we've tried. Upgrade-releases of
neideck> the translator should push this slowly upwards over the next
neideck> few quarters. I haven't seen SysMark results for "translated"
neideck> machines yet.

Equivalent results (for other Alpha CPUs) have also been reported in
some magazines, so they sound rather plausible.

However there is a terrible marketing trap in successful emulation: that
it essentially kills any incentive for application developers to target
the native environment. OS/2 is in many ways indeed a better Windows
than Windows; so it is basically pointless, at least in marketing/volume
terms, to develop for OS/2 itself.

If FX!32 makes an Alpha a better (or even almost as good) Pentium than a
Pentium, developers will have zero _marketing_ reasons to target the
Alpha ISA: suppose that FX!32 really delivers something it is already
pretty close to, apparently, that is Pentium applications running on
Alpha at 50% of native speed: there is then little reason (a mere
doubling in speed) to sell both Pentium and Alpha binaries, with all the
attendant distribution and support problems.

Microsoft is having exactly the same problem at another level, with
WIN16 vs. WIN32 (or rather its many incompatible variants): given that
Windows 95 runs rather well WIN16 applications, and that Windows 3
cannot run WIN32 ones (WIN32S is not quite enough), the market-oriented
developer will look at the enormous installed base of WIN16 systems (and
the cash-cow upgrade market), the much smaller installed base of WIN32
systems, and be reluctant to release WIN32 versions of products...

After all this has bitten Intel itself: they were fully expecting for
WIN32 to be dominant, and thus the Pro's relative slowness with WIN16
apps.



